  "rust_signature_advice": "Do not model this as `str::parse`. Model it as libstdc++ `strtol` + range check, over BYTES, with a two-variant error.\n\nRecommended shape:\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\nenum StoiError { InvalidArgument, OutOfRange }\n\n/// Byte-for-byte equivalent of libstdc++ std::stoi(s) with base 10.\n/// Takes bytes because argv on Linux is arbitrary bytes, not UTF-8.\nfn stoi(s: &[u8]) -> Result<i32, StoiError>\n```\n\nWhy each choice matters:\n\n- `&[u8]`, not `&str`: verified that argv[2] = 0x35 0xFF returns 5 with no error. Read args with `std::env::args_os()` and `std::os::unix::ffi::OsStrExt::as_bytes()`. `std::env::args()` PANICS on non-UTF-8 and would turn a success case into a crash with the wrong message.\n- Two error variants, not one: the two paths produce different stderr text, and callers must be able to emit the right one. Do not collapse them, and do not reuse `std::num::ParseIntError` — its `Empty`/`InvalidDigit`/`PosOverflow`/`NegOverflow` kinds do not partition the same way (`\"1x\"` is `InvalidDigit` in Rust but a SUCCESS returning 1 in C++).\n- Return `Result`, not `Option` and not a panic: keep the abort decision at the call site so the exact stderr bytes and SIGABRT are emitted by one small helper.\n- If you ever need `std::stoi`'s two-arg form, add `fn stoi_pos(s: &[u8]) -> Result<(i32, usize), StoiError>` returning the consumed length; this program does not need it.\n- Accumulate in `i64` (mirrors LP64 `long` inside strtol) with `saturating_mul`/`saturating_add`, apply the sign, then range-check against `i32::MIN..=i32::MAX`. Never accumulate the magnitude in `i32` or `u32`.\n- A convenience wrapper for the caller, so the abort text lives in exactly one place:\n\n```rust\nfn die(what: &str) -> ! {\n    eprint!(\"terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::{what}'\\n  what():  stoi\\n\");\n    std::process::abort()   // SIGABRT -> shell-visible 134; process::exit(134) is NOT equivalent\n}\n```\n\n- The main-level guard should be `if args.len() > 1 && args[1].as_bytes() == b\"--a\"`, then handle a missing `args[2]` by emitting the distinct logic_error text (`basic_string::_S_construct null not valid`, with no `stoi` in it) and aborting.\n- `if let Ok(n) = ...` / `unwrap_or(15)` style fallbacks are wrong: a bad value must abort, not silently fall back to 15.",
  "pitfalls": [
   "`\"1x\".parse::<i32>()` is Err, but stoi returns 1. Rust's parse requires the WHOLE string to be a number; stoi consumes a maximal prefix and silently discards the rest. This is the single biggest behavioral divergence and it affects \"3x\", \"12abc\", \"5 5\", \"1.9\", \"1e3\", \"1,000\", \"1_0\", \"1+\", \"-1-\".",
   "`\" 1\".parse::<i32>()` is Err, but stoi returns 1. Rust's parse rejects ALL surrounding whitespace; stoi skips leading whitespace and ignores trailing whitespace. Calling `.trim()` first is a tempting fix but it is subtly wrong in two ways (see next two items).",
   "`str::trim()` uses Unicode `char::is_whitespace`, which strips U+00A0 NBSP, U+2000-series spaces, U+3000 ideographic space, U+2028/U+2029. stoi rejects ALL of these (verified: NBSP+'1' and U+3000+'1' both throw invalid_argument). Trimming would turn a required abort into a success returning 1.",
   "`u8::is_ascii_whitespace()` / `char::is_ascii_whitespace()` EXCLUDE vertical tab (0x0B), but C `isspace` in the C locale includes it. Verified: \"\\u{0b}1\" returns 1. Hand-roll the set `{0x20, 0x09, 0x0A, 0x0B, 0x0C, 0x0D}` — using Rust's ascii-whitespace helper makes \"\\u{0b}1\" wrongly abort.",
   "`str::trim_ascii()` also omits vertical tab, same bug as above.",
   "Parsing the magnitude into `i32` and then negating overflows on INT_MIN. \"-2147483648\" is a VALID input that must return -2147483648, while \"2147483648\" must throw out_of_range. The accepted range is asymmetric; the sign must be applied in a wider type BEFORE the range check.",
   "Very long digit strings must SATURATE, not wrap. 5000 nines throws out_of_range; a wrapping `i64` accumulator could land on a small in-range value and silently succeed. Use `saturating_mul(10).saturating_add(d)` or bail out early once the accumulator exceeds i32 range.",
   "Overflow detection must beat junk-suffix tolerance. \"2147483648x\" throws out_of_range — you cannot short-circuit \"stop at first non-digit, then parse what you collected\" in a way that drops the overflow check, and you cannot decide \"has junk, so just take the prefix\" before checking range.",
   "Leading zeros do not rescue overflow: \"0000002147483648\" still throws out_of_range. Do not strip zeros and then compare the remaining digit-string LENGTH against 10 as an overflow heuristic.",
   "No hex prefix handling. `i32::from_str_radix(s, 16)` or any `0x` sniffing is wrong: \"0x1\" is 0, \"0X1A\" is 0, \"-0x10\" is 0. Base is hard-coded 10 and 'x' is just the terminating junk byte.",
   "No octal handling. \"08\" and \"09\" parse fine as 8 and 9 (verified decisively — base 8 would have yielded 0 and printed 0), and \"010\" is 10 not 8. A port that mimics `strtol(s, NULL, 0)` auto-base detection would be wrong on all of these.",
   "No locale grouping. The program never calls `setlocale`, so LC_ALL/LC_NUMERIC/LANG are inert (verified: LC_ALL=de_DE.UTF-8 with \"5,000,000\" still yields 5). Do not implement thousands separators.",
   "Only ASCII digits count. Arabic-Indic \\u{663}, fullwidth \\u{ff13}, superscript \\u{b2}, \\u{bd}, Roman \\u{2163} all throw invalid_argument. A port using `char::is_numeric()`, `char::is_alphanumeric()`, or `char::to_digit(10)` over `chars()` will wrongly accept some of these (`to_digit` is ASCII-only, but `is_numeric` is not) — restrict to the byte range 0x30..=0x39.",
   "At most ONE sign, with no whitespace after it. \"--1\", \"++1\", \"+-1\", \"-+1\", \"- 1\", \" + 1\" all throw invalid_argument. A loop that consumes signs, or a whitespace-skip repeated after the sign, breaks these.",
   "`std::env::args()` panics on non-UTF-8 argv; the C++ program happily returns 5 for bytes 0x35 0xFF. Use `args_os()` + `OsStrExt::as_bytes()`.",
   "Exit status must come from a real SIGABRT. Uncaught C++ exceptions call `std::terminate` -> `abort()`, giving signal 6 (shell-visible 134) plus a core dump. `std::process::exit(134)` produces the same number in `$?` but no signal and no core dump, so anything inspecting `WIFSIGNALED`/wait status sees a difference. Use `std::process::abort()`. Also remember a Rust `panic!` gives exit 101 with a totally different message.",
   "The error text is byte-exact and easy to get wrong: two spaces of indent before `what():`, then TWO spaces before `stoi`, i.e. `\\\"  what():  stoi\\\\n\\\"`. Both messages are `stoi` (not `stoi: ...` and not the input value), and stdout stays completely empty. Verified with `od -c`.",
   "Missing argv[2] is a THIRD, different failure: `std::logic_error` with `basic_string::_S_construct null not valid`. In Rust `args_os().nth(2)` is simply `None`, so it is easy to accidentally fall back to 15 or emit the stoi message instead.",
   "argv[1] is compared with exact bytes: `\\\"--a \\\"` (trailing space) does NOT match, so stoi is skipped and count stays 15. Do not trim or case-fold argv[1].",
   "Downstream of stoi: for large accepted counts, `i * 12` overflows i32 once i > 178956970. C++ signed overflow is UB but in practice wraps; a Rust debug build PANICS with a different message and exit 101. Use `wrapping_mul(12)` (or i64 keys) if you want identical behavior for counts near INT_MAX — though in practice those runs die of allocation failure first.",
   "`count / 2` happens to agree between C++ and Rust (both truncate toward zero) and does not overflow even at INT_MIN, so no special handling is needed there — but do not \"fix\" it into a floor-division helper."
  ]
 },
 "argv": {
  "argv_mapping": "One-to-one, same indices, no offset. C++ `argv[0]` == Rust `std::env::args().next()` (index 0) == program name/path; C++ `argv[1]` == `env::args().nth(1)`; C++ `argv[2]` == `env::args().nth(2)`. C++ `argc` == `env::args().count()`, so `argc > 1` is exactly `env::args().nth(1).is_some()` (equivalently `args().count() > 1`, or `args().len() > 1` on `ArgsOs`).\n\nVERIFIED by observation (only `count<=0` vs `count>0` is observable in stdout, so I used `0` as the probe value):\n- `./test15_executable \"--a\" \"0\"` -> `0`  (two separate argv entries: argv[1]=\"--a\", argv[2]=\"0\" -> count=0)\n- `./test15_executable \"--a 0\"`   -> `1`  (ONE argv entry \"--a 0\" != \"--a\" -> count stays 15). This proves the program sees the pre-split argv vector, not a joined command line — identical to Rust's `env::args()`.\n- `./test15_executable \"--a0\"` -> `1`; `./test15_executable \"--a \" \"0\"` -> `1`; `./test15_executable \" --a\" \"0\"` -> `1` (comparison is exact byte equality, no trimming).\n- `./test15_executable \"\" 0` -> `1` and `./test15_executable \"\" --a 0` -> `1` (the \"--a\" test is applied ONLY to index 1; a \"--a\" at index 2+ is never inspected as a flag).\n- argv[0] content is irrelevant: invoked as `./test15_executable --a 0` and `/workspace/dataset/test15_executable --a 0` both print `0`.\n\nTwo porting hazards:\n1. C++ `argv[argc]` is guaranteed NULL, and `std::stoi(argv[2])` therefore constructs `std::string(nullptr)` when only `--a` is given -> throws (see failure_modes). In Rust `args().nth(2)` is simply `None`; you must synthesize that abort explicitly.\n2. C++ compares raw bytes. `std::env::args()` PANICS on non-UTF-8 arguments, while the C++ binary handles them fine — VERIFIED: `./test15_executable $'\\xff'` prints `1` and exits 0, and `./test15_executable --a $'5\\xff'` prints `1` (stoi reads the \"5\" prefix). For byte-exact fidelity use `std::env::args_os()` and compare `OsStr` bytes (e.g. `os.as_encoded_bytes() == b\"--a\"` or `os == OsStr::new(\"--a\")`), then parse argv[2] from `to_string_lossy()`/bytes rather than `args()`.",
  "branch_conditions": [
