  "equal_range_semantics": "tsl::hopscotch_map is a UNIQUE-key (std::unordered_map-like) container, so equal_range degenerates to a \"0-or-1 element\" range, exactly like std::unordered_map::equal_range.\n\nContract, expressed in terms of find()/end():\n  auto it = find(key);\n  if (it == end()) return {end(), end()};   // key ABSENT -> empty range\n  else             return {it, std::next(it)}; // key PRESENT -> half-open range covering exactly that one element\n\n- Key PRESENT: range.first points at the single element whose key compares equal; range.second is the immediate successor position in iteration order (which is end() when the found element happens to be last in iteration order). Therefore std::distance(range.first, range.second) == 1 and the counting loop `for (it = first; it != second; ++it) num_in_range++` executes its body exactly once -> yields 1.\n- Key ABSENT: range.first == range.second (both end(), or more generally two equal iterators), the loop condition `it != second` is false on entry -> yields 0.\n- Consequence: num_in_range is always identical to map.count(key), i.e. it is a boolean-valued 0/1 presence test. Multiplicity can never exceed 1 because insert() on a duplicate key is a no-op that returns {existing_iterator, false}. So the \"range\" concept from multimaps (where equal_range can span N>1 equal keys) never produces N>1 here. This is the whole point of the test: it pins equal_range to unique-key semantics.\n- Bucket-order caveat that does NOT affect the count: hopscotch hashing means iteration order is unspecified/implementation-defined, so WHICH element range.second names varies with count and hash layout, but the DISTANCE is invariantly 1. A faithful port must not try to reproduce C++ iteration order; it only has to reproduce distance == 1 (present) / 0 (absent).",
  "expected_output_rule": "Keys inserted are exactly {0, 1, ..., count-1} (the loop `for (i=0; i<count; i++)` runs max(count,0) times). The queried key is `count / 2` using C++ INTEGER division, which truncates toward ZERO (so -1/2 == 0, -3/2 == -1, -1000000/2 == -500000).\n\nCLOSED FORM:\n    printed = (count >= 1) ? 1 : 0\nequivalently `if count > 0 { 1 } else { 0 }`  -- i.e. `usize::from(count > 0)`.\n\nProof of the two branches:\n- count <= 0 (includes all negatives and 0): the insert loop body never executes, so the map is EMPTY. Any query key is absent -> equal_range gives an empty range -> prints 0. (Note the queried key is still computed, e.g. count=-3 queries -1, count=-1 queries 0, count=0 queries 0, but an empty map contains nothing, so the value of the key is irrelevant.)\n- count >= 1: we need 0 <= count/2 <= count-1 so that count/2 is one of the inserted keys.\n    * Lower bound: count >= 1 > 0 so floor(count/2) >= 0.\n    * Upper bound: floor(count/2) <= count-1 holds for every count >= 1 (count=1: 0 <= 0; count=2: 1 <= 1; and for count >= 2 the gap count-1-floor(count/2) = ceil(count/2)-1 >= 0 and grows).\n  So the key is ALWAYS present, and by unique-key semantics the range has exactly one element -> prints 1.\n\nPer-case table requested:\n- count < 0            -> 0   (empty map; key = trunc-toward-zero count/2, irrelevant)\n- count == 0           -> 0   (empty map, key 0 absent)\n- count == 1           -> 1   (keys {0}, key 1/2 == 0, present -- the tight boundary case)\n- count == 2           -> 1   (keys {0,1}, key 2/2 == 1, present -- the other tight boundary: key equals the LAST inserted key, so range.second may be end())\n- odd count >= 1       -> 1   (key = (count-1)/2, strictly interior for count >= 3)\n- even count >= 2      -> 1   (key = count/2, interior for count >= 4)\n- large count (up to 9,999,999 verified; holds for all count up to INT_MAX) -> 1\n- default, no args (count = 15) -> 1\n\nPARITY AND MAGNITUDE ARE IRRELEVANT. The only discriminator is emptiness of the map, i.e. sign/zero-ness of count. There is no count >= 1 that prints anything other than 1, and no count <= 0 that prints anything other than 0. A correct Rust port therefore prints exactly two possible values, 0 and 1.\n\nArgument-parsing behavior the port must also match (observed):\n- No args, or argv[1] != \"--a\" (e.g. `--b 7`): count stays 15 -> prints 1, exit 0.\n- `--a <n>`: count = stoi(n).\n- `--a` with NO argv[2]: C++ constructs std::string from a NULL pointer -> throws std::logic_error \"basic_string::_S_construct null not valid\" -> abort, exit 134, nothing printed on stdout. (Rust: this is a panic/abort path, not a 0.)\n- `--a abc` (no leading digits): std::stoi throws std::invalid_argument -> abort, exit 134.\n- `--a 9xyz`: std::stoi parses the leading digits and IGNORES the trailing junk -> count = 9 -> prints 1, exit 0. A naive Rust `str::parse::<i32>()` would FAIL here, so the port needs stoi-style prefix parsing to be faithful.",
