  "perf_notes": "TARGET: C++ does 10,000,000 inserts + one equal_range in ~0.40 s (~40 ns/insert amortised, measured in the spec's evidence). Budget for this design: 0.3-0.8 s in `cargo build --release` / `rustc -O`. The dominant cost in both implementations is identical — one random access into a ~200 MB table per insert — so the achievable ratio is close to 1x, and the risk is entirely in constant factors.\n\nSIZING. 12 bytes/bucket for `Option<(i32,i32)>`, power-of-two buckets, 3/4 load threshold. n = 10M -> 16,777,216 buckets (threshold 12.58M) -> 201 MB steady, ~302 MB transient while the last doubling holds old+new. Identical footprint to a hopscotch bucket array of `pair<int,int>` + 32-bit bitmap. Load right after a doubling is 0.375 and just before the next is 0.75.\n\nPROBE COUNTS (linear probing, well-mixed hash). Unsuccessful search (= insert) costs ~(1 + 1/(1-a)^2)/2 probes: 1.8 at a=0.375, 8.5 at a=0.75, averaging ~3.7 across a doubling cycle. Successful search costs (1 + 1/(1-a))/2: 1.8 at a=0.6. Crucially those extra probes are CONSECUTIVE slots — 5 buckets per 64-byte line — so a probe run of 4 is typically still one cache miss. This is the reason plain linear probing is competitive with hopscotch here despite having no bitmap: hopscotch's bitmap buys a bounded window, not fewer cache lines.\n\nREHASH. The doubling series moves <2n entries total, each a hash + a linear scan for the first empty slot with no key comparisons. Allocation is `Vec::with_capacity` + `resize_with(n, || None)`; the 201 MB fill is a ~20-40 ms memset-class cost, amortised.\n\nHASHER. `MixingHasher` overrides `write_i32/write_u32/write_u64/write_usize` so an i32 key costs one xor-multiply-shift chain (~3-4 ns) instead of the default 4-byte `write` loop. Do NOT default to `RandomState`: SipHash-1-3 on a single i32 is roughly 10-20 ns including per-hash state setup, which alone could add ~0.15 s at 10M and would visibly widen the gap to C++. `RandomState` stays available through `with_hasher` for anyone who wants HashDoS resistance. `BuildIdentityHasher` (the exact `std::hash<int>` behaviour) is also available and is actually optimal for this workload — keys 0..n-1 land in distinct buckets with zero collisions — but it is not the default because it degenerates catastrophically for strided key sets.\n\nBOUNDS CHECKS. Every probe indexes `self.slots[i]` with `i = ... & self.mask`; LLVM generally cannot prove `mask+1 == slots.len()` so a compare-and-branch survives. That is a predictable branch against an already-hot length field, ~1 cycle next to a ~80 ns cache miss. Keeping it (rather than reaching for `get_unchecked`) is a deliberate zero-unsafe trade; if a benchmark ever proves otherwise, the safe fix is to hoist `let slots = &self.slots[..]` and iterate a subslice, not to add `unsafe`.\n\nBUILD FLAGS MATTER MORE THAN THE ALGORITHM. `cargo build` (dev, opt-level 0) will be roughly 10-20x slower and may take several seconds at 10M — always benchmark `cargo build --release`. The standalone path must be benchmarked as `rustc -O test15.rs`; bare `rustc test15.rs` is opt-level 0 AND has overflow checks on. `[profile.release]` should carry `opt-level = 3`, `codegen-units = 1`, `lto = \"thin\"`.\n\nSMALL-INPUT COSTS. Default count = 15: one 8-bucket allocation, one growth to 16, ~15 inserts, a find and a short successor scan — microseconds; process startup dominates. `count <= 0`: zero allocations at all (the map never allocates), `equal_range` returns on the `slots.is_empty()` guard.\n\nIF IT COMES IN SLOW, the escalation ladder in order of (benefit / added risk): (1) lower the load threshold to 1/2 — one constant in growth.rs, costs ~2x memory; (2) split the tag out into a parallel `Vec<u8>` control array so `(i32,i32)` pairs pack 8 per cache line — needs `MaybeUninit` and `unsafe`, so only under measurement; (3) cache 7 hash bits in that control byte to skip key comparisons — only helps expensive keys. None of these is needed for correctness and none should be done speculatively.",
  "risks": [
   "FIDELITY GIVEN UP #1 — no bounded worst case. tsl::hopscotch_map guarantees a lookup touches at most NEIGHBORHOOD_SIZE buckets (a single bitmap test plus a short window), spilling to an overflow list when a neighborhood saturates. Linear probing has no such bound: with an adversarial or degenerate hash it degrades to O(n) per operation, and even with a good hash the tail latency at 0.75 load is a run of tens of slots. This is invisible to the ported program (sequential integer keys, mixing hasher) but it IS the headline property of hopscotch hashing, and this design does not have it. Say so in the module doc rather than letting a reader assume otherwise.",
   "FIDELITY GIVEN UP #2 — different iteration order, so `equal_range(...).1` names a different element than the C++ does. Only the DISTANCE (1 if present, 0 if absent) is invariant, and only the distance is observable through this program's stdout. Any future ported test that prints `range.second->first`, compares iteration order, or depends on bucket layout would diverge. Unobservable here; unfixable in principle without reimplementing the real algorithm.",
   "FIDELITY GIVEN UP #3 — erase moves other elements. Backward-shift deletion relocates entries to repair the probe-run invariant, so removing X invalidates a cursor to unrelated Y; hopscotch's erase clears one bucket and clears one bitmap bit, leaving other elements put. The ported program never erases, so this cannot affect output, but a port of any erase-plus-iterate test would break. The alternative (tombstones) preserves positions but adds a third slot state to every probe loop and needs a compaction policy — rejected as more total risk than it removes.",
   "FIDELITY GIVEN UP #4 — none of hopscotch's tuning surface exists: no NeighborhoodSize/StoreHash template parameters, no overflow_size(), no min_load_factor / rehash-policy choice, no prime growth policy (power-of-two only). If sibling tests in this dataset touch those members, this map will need real additions rather than a rename.",
   "COMPILE RISK, ALREADY REALISED IN THIS TREE — trait-bound stratification. I compiled the existing /output/src/tsl/ hopscotch attempt and it fails with six E0599s (`end_pos`, `pos_after`, `entry_at`, `len` \"exist but trait bounds were not satisfied\") because those methods sit in an `impl<K: Hash + Eq, S: BuildHasher>` block while `Iter`/`EqualRange` are generic over bare `K, V, S`. The mitigation is structural and must be designed in from line one: every cursor/iteration/storage primitive is a bound-free free function over `&[Slot<K,V>]` in table.rs, the unbounded inherent impl only forwards to those, and `K: Hash + Eq, S: BuildHasher` appears ONLY on the impl block containing operations that actually hash.",
   "PANIC RISK #1 (the `count <= 0` requirement) — the never-allocated map. `mask` is 0 and `slots` is empty, so any `self.slots[hash & self.mask]` panics with index-out-of-bounds, exit 101, where C++ prints `0`. Mitigation: an `if self.slots.is_empty()` early return at the top of find/insert-probe/equal_range/remove, plus explicit unit tests for `equal_range` on a default-constructed map and for `count = i32::MIN`. Related: never write `count as usize` anywhere (i32::MIN as usize is 18446744071562067968).",
   "PANIC RISK #2 — build-dependent integer overflow. Verified with `rustc --print cfg`: bare `rustc test15.rs` runs at opt-level 0, which turns `debug_assertions` (and hence `-C overflow-checks`) ON, while `cargo build --release` turns them off. So a literal `i * 12` panics with 'attempt to multiply with overflow' in the standalone build at i >= 178_956_971 but silently wraps like C++ in the cargo build. Every arithmetic op that can overflow — the `* 12`, the hasher multiplies, the mix64 chain — must use `wrapping_mul`/`wrapping_add` explicitly. Unit-test the wrap directly rather than relying on a 3-billion-key run to expose it.",
   "EDITION RISK — bare `rustc test15.rs` compiles as EDITION 2015 (verified: this toolchain accepts `let async = 1;` from the default invocation), while cargo compiles the same bytes as 2021. Verified hard errors under 2015 that look perfectly fine under 2021: `x.try_into()` and `Vec::from_iter(..)` (TryInto/TryFrom/FromIterator are 2021-prelude-only). Also latent: explicit `.into_iter()` on arrays resolves to slice-of-refs in 2015, and 2021's disjoint closure captures can make a closure that borrows one field of `self` while another is mutably borrowed compile only under 2021. Mitigation: import every trait explicitly, avoid try_into/from_iter, avoid array `.into_iter()`, avoid clever closures — and add `rustc test15.rs -o /tmp/t15_2015` to the verification checklist alongside `cargo build --release`, because cargo alone will never catch this class of break.",
   "STRUCTURAL RISK — the temptation to add a `[lib]`. A lib + bin split would make `rustc test15.rs` impossible (no `--extern` on that command line), and a stray `src/main.rs` or `src/bin/*.rs` would create a second auto-discovered bin target colliding with the explicit one. Keep exactly one `[[bin]]`, keep `src/` free of `main.rs`/`lib.rs`/`bin/`, and consider `autobins = false` as belt-and-braces. Note also that `rustc test15.rs` drops a binary named `test15` right next to `test15.rs`; always pass `-o /tmp/...` in scripts so the package root stays clean.",
   "SILENT-CORRUPTION RISK — `Pos` is a bare index with no generation tag, so a cursor used after a rehash points at a DIFFERENT live element rather than dangling: a wrong answer instead of a crash. C++ iterators are equally invalidated but tend to fail loudly. Cheap mitigation if it matters: a `#[cfg(debug_assertions)]` generation counter on the map and on `Pos`, asserted on every dereference; it must not change `Pos`'s Copy/PartialEq behaviour in release.",
   "CORRECTNESS RISK — backward-shift erase is the one genuinely subtle piece of code in the design, and the cyclic membership test `h in (i, j]` is easy to get off by one (the classic bug leaves an unreachable entry that `find` can never see again, which shows up only as a missing key much later). Mitigations: write the textbook two-branch form, cross-check it against the branch-free `((h - i - 1) & mask) < ((j - i) & mask)` form in a test, and run an exhaustive randomised insert/erase/lookup differential against `std::collections::HashMap` on a 16-bucket table with a deliberately collision-prone hasher.",
   "COMPILE-TRAP — `vec![None; n]` requires `K: Clone + V: Clone` and will fail for a general map; use `Vec::with_capacity(n)` + `resize_with(n, || None)`. Similarly, the natural `match &mut self.slots[i] { None => { self.slots[i] = ... } ... }` insert loop trips borrowck; the two-phase immutable-probe-then-single-write formulation avoids it without `unsafe`.",
   "DETERMINISTIC-HASHER RISK — `MixingHasher` is seedless, so a caller who controls keys can force collisions. Honest framing: the C++ being ported uses `std::hash<int>`, which is the identity function and therefore even more attackable, so this is not a regression; `with_hasher(RandomState::new())` is available for anyone who needs it.",
   "ALLOCATION-FAILURE DIVERGENCE — at counts near INT_MAX the C++ dies with `std::bad_alloc` (or the OOM killer); Rust aborts with `memory allocation of N bytes failed` on stderr, also via SIGABRT/134. Same signal and same (empty) stdout, different stderr text. Out of scope for 'byte-identical stdout on valid inputs', but worth stating so nobody 'fixes' it later. Related: keep `panic = \"unwind\"` in the release profile — `panic = \"abort\"` would make an accidental Rust panic masquerade as the C++ abort (SIGABRT/134) instead of the loud, obviously-wrong exit 101.",
   "PERF RISK — the estimates above are unmeasured. The two most likely misses are defaulting to `RandomState` (adds ~10-20 ns/insert) and benchmarking a dev build by accident. Mitigation: a scripted check that runs `--a 10000000` under both `cargo build --release` and `rustc -O` and compares wall time against /workspace/dataset/test15_executable directly, before any micro-optimisation is attempted."
  ]
 },
 {
  "approach_name": "Layered `tsl` + `cxx` split with `#[path]`-included modules under a package-root bin (three layers: generic hopscotch core → map facade → C++ runtime shims → thin entry)",
  "module_layout": [
   "/output/Cargo.toml -> Package manifest ONLY. `edition = \"2021\"`, empty `[dependencies]`, `[profile.release] opt-level = 3`, and a single explicit target: `[[bin]] name = \"test15\", path = \"test15.rs\"`. Deliberately NO `[lib]` section, NO `src/lib.rs`, NO `src/main.rs` — cargo's target auto-discovery then contributes nothing, so the package-root `test15.rs` is the one and only crate root under both build commands.",
   "/output/test15.rs -> The crate root for BOTH `cargo build --release` and `rustc test15.rs`. Contains exactly three things and no logic: (1) the crate-level doc comment, (2) the module wiring `#[path = \"src/tsl/mod.rs\"] mod tsl;` and `#[path = \"src/cxx/mod.rs\"] mod cxx;` and `#[path = \"src/app.rs\"] mod app;`, (3) a ~15-line `fn main()`. VERIFIED: `#[path]` on a module declared in the crate root resolves relative to the directory containing the crate-root FILE, not the process cwd — I compiled the same tree as `rustc test15.rs` from the package dir AND as `rustc /tmp/dbe/test15.rs` from `/tmp`, and both linked all five module files and produced identical output. This is what makes the dual-build requirement work verbatim.",
   "/output/src/tsl/mod.rs -> Facade for the `tsl` namespace. Nothing but `pub mod hash; pub mod hopscotch_hash; pub mod hopscotch_map;`, the `pub use` re-exports that let callers write `tsl::HopscotchMap`, a module doc comment mapping each C++ name to its Rust counterpart, and `#![allow(dead_code, unused_imports)]` for the whole layer. NOTE: because this file is reached via `#[path = \".../mod.rs\"]`, it acts as a directory owner, so its children are plain `mod` declarations resolving to siblings in `src/tsl/` — the `#[path]` attribute is needed only twice, in the crate root. Verified.",
   "/output/src/tsl/hash.rs -> The `std::hash` substitute layer; knows nothing about tables. `IdentityHasher` + `BuildIdentityHasher` reproduce libstdc++ `std::hash<integral>`, which is `static_cast<size_t>` of the value (so `write_i32` should sign-extend: `self.state = i as i64 as u64`, matching `(size_t)(-1) == 0xFFFF_FFFF_FFFF_FFFF`; under power-of-two low-bit masking this is indistinguishable from `i as u32 as u64`, but sign extension is the faithful transcription). `write` falls back to FNV-1a so `&str`/`[u8]` keys still spread. `MixingHasher` + `BuildMixingHasher` provide a murmur3 `fmix64` full-avalanche finalizer for hostile key distributions. Both are `BuildHasher` impls, so the table is pluggable.",
   "/output/src/tsl/hopscotch_hash.rs -> The generic hopscotch core, `HopscotchHash<K, V, S = BuildIdentityHasher>` where `K: Hash + Eq, S: BuildHasher`. Owns the bucket array, the neighbourhood bitmaps, the overflow vector, the load factor, growth/rehash, and all `Pos` arithmetic. Every public method is expressed in terms of `Pos` (a plain index newtype) — no lifetimes and no iterator types appear at this layer, which is what keeps the borrow checker out of the algorithmic code. It has no notion of 'map' vs 'set' semantics beyond storing a `(K, V)` tuple per slot.",
   "/output/src/tsl/hopscotch_map.rs -> The `tsl::hopscotch_map` layer: a newtype wrapping `HopscotchHash` that adds (a) the C++-shaped surface the ported test names — `insert((k, v))` taking ONE tuple to mirror `map.insert({i, i * 12})`, `equal_range`, `count`, `find`, `begin`, `end` — and (b) the safe borrow-checked iterator types `Iter`, `EqualRange`, `Keys`, `Values` that convert a raw `Pos` into `(&K, &V)` yields. This is the only `tsl` module the entry file talks to.",
   "/output/src/cxx/mod.rs -> Facade for the C++ runtime-compat layer: `pub mod stoi; pub mod argv; pub mod terminate;` plus re-exports. Physically separate from `src/tsl/` because it is a different kind of thing — emulation of libstdc++/libc behavior rather than a container — and because it must stay dependency-free of `tsl` (the dependency arrows run entry -> {tsl, cxx, app}, app -> tsl, cxx -> nothing; no cycles).",
   "/output/src/cxx/stoi.rs -> A pure `std::stoi` clone over BYTES: `pub fn stoi(s: &[u8]) -> Result<i32, StoiError>` and `pub enum StoiError { InvalidArgument, OutOfRange }`, plus a private `fn is_c_space(b: u8) -> bool` matching the exact C-locale set `{0x20, 0x09, 0x0A, 0x0B, 0x0C, 0x0D}`. Zero I/O, zero process control, no panics — which is precisely why the ~90 vectors in the behavioral spec can all become `#[test]` cases in this file. Also the natural home for `pub fn stoi_pos(s: &[u8]) -> Result<(i32, usize), StoiError>` (the two-arg C++ overload) even though the program never calls it.",
   "/output/src/cxx/argv.rs -> argv plumbing and the program's option gate. `pub struct Argv(Vec<Vec<u8>>)` captured via `std::env::args_os()` + `OsStrExt::as_bytes()` (NEVER `env::args()`, which panics on non-UTF-8 where the C++ binary happily returns 5 for bytes `0x35 0xFF`), with `from_env()`, `argc()`, `get(i) -> Option<&[u8]>`. `pub fn resolve_count(argv: &Argv) -> Result<i32, Fault>` encodes the gate `argc > 1 && argv[1] == b\"--a\"` and returns `Ok(15)` when the gate fails, `Err(Fault::NullString)` when `argv[1] == b\"--a\"` but `argv[2]` is absent, and `Err(Fault::Stoi(e))` otherwise. `pub enum Fault { NullString, Stoi(StoiError) }`.",
   "/output/src/cxx/terminate.rs -> libstdc++ `std::terminate` emulation, the single place any byte of stderr is produced. `pub fn terminate_with(exception: &str, what: &str) -> !` writes `format!(\"terminate called after throwing an instance of '{exception}'\\n  what():  {what}\\n\")` (two-space indent, TWO spaces after the colon) then calls `std::process::abort()`. Three named wrappers hide the strings: `logic_error_null_string()`, `stoi_invalid_argument()`, `stoi_out_of_range()`. VERIFIED byte-identical: my Rust `eprint!` + `abort()` produced a stderr file that `cmp`'d equal to the C++ binary's 87-byte stderr, both dying `Aborted (core dumped)` with shell status 134.",
   "/output/src/app.rs -> The ported program as a PURE function, so the observable output is unit-testable in-process without spawning a binary: `pub fn build_map(count: i32) -> HopscotchMap<i32, i32>` and `pub fn num_in_range(count: i32) -> usize`. `main` then reduces to: `let argv = Argv::from_env(); let count = match resolve_count(&argv) { Ok(c) => c, Err(f) => f.terminate() }; println!(\"{}\", app::num_in_range(count));`. `build_map` deliberately does NOT pre-reserve capacity, so the rehash path is exercised exactly as the C++ exercises it (this is what the spec's 4095/4096/4097 and 65535/65536/65537 probes are designed to catch).",
   "TESTS: keep every `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` INLINE in the module it covers. VERIFIED that `cargo test` compiles the bin target with `--test` and collects tests from every `#[path]`-included module (my 5-file replica reported `running 4 tests` with names `tsl::hash::tests::id`, `tsl::hopscotch_hash::tests::p`, `tsl::tests::smoke`, `compat::stoi::tests::junk`). Corollary and trap: a `/output/tests/` integration directory would NOT work, because with no `[lib]` target there is no `test15` crate to `use`; such a file would have to repeat the `#[path]` includes itself."
  ],
  "core_data_structures": "LAYER 1 — the bucket (`hopscotch_hash.rs`):\n\n```rust\npub const NEIGHBOURHOOD_SIZE: usize = 32;   // bitmap is a u32\npub const INITIAL_BUCKET_COUNT: usize = 16;\npub const DEFAULT_MAX_LOAD_FACTOR: f32 = 0.9;\n\npub struct Bucket<K, V> {\n    neighbourhood: u32,          // bit d set <=> slot (home + d) holds an entry whose home is this bucket\n    entry: Option<(K, V)>,       // None == vacant slot\n}\n```\nTwo fields, not three: the bitmap is meaningful only for a *home* bucket, the `Option` only for a *storage* slot, and one bucket plays both roles independently. For `K = V = i32`, `Option<(i32, i32)>` is 12 bytes and the struct is 16 bytes — exactly four buckets per cache line, so a 32-slot neighbourhood scan touches at most 8 lines and in practice 1-2 because the bitmap tells you which slots to visit.\n\nLAYER 1 — the table:\n\n```rust\npub struct HopscotchHash<K, V, S = BuildIdentityHasher> {\n    buckets: Vec<Bucket<K, V>>,  // len == bucket_count + NEIGHBOURHOOD_SIZE - 1\n    overflow: Vec<(K, V)>,       // unbounded fallback; keeps insert total\n    len: usize,                  // live entries, buckets + overflow\n    mask: usize,                 // bucket_count - 1; bucket_count is always a power of two\n    hash_builder: S,\n    max_load_factor: f32,\n}\n```\nThree invariants carry the whole design: (1) `bucket_count` is a power of two so the modulo is `hash & mask`; (2) the vector is over-allocated by `NEIGHBOURHOOD_SIZE - 1` trailing slots so a neighbourhood starting at the last home bucket never wraps — no modular index arithmetic anywhere, and those tail slots are legal storage but never home buckets; (3) `buckets[h].neighbourhood` bit `d` is set iff `buckets[h + d].entry.is_some()` and that entry's home is `h`, which is what makes lookup touch only set bits.\n\nPOSITIONS — the C++ iterator substitute:\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]\npub struct Pos(usize);\n```\nOne flat, totally-ordered index space over both containers: `0 .. slots()` indexes `buckets` (where `slots() == buckets.len()`), `slots() .. slots() + overflow.len()` indexes `overflow`, and `end_pos() == Pos(slots() + overflow.len())` is one past the last. Because `Pos` is `Copy + PartialEq`, the ported loop's `first != second` and `++it` map onto `!=` and `pos_after` with no unsafe code and no lifetime entanglement — this is the single decision that keeps the core algorithms readable.\n\nLAYER 2 — the map facade and its iterators (`hopscotch_map.rs`):\n\n```rust\npub struct HopscotchMap<K, V, S = BuildIdentityHasher> { table: HopscotchHash<K, V, S> }\n\npub struct Iter<'a, K, V, S>       { map: &'a HopscotchMap<K, V, S>, cur: Pos, stop: Pos }\npub struct EqualRange<'a, K, V, S> { inner: Iter<'a, K, V, S> }   // same shape, distinct type for intent\n```\nAn iterator is just a borrow plus two `Pos` values, i.e. a literal C++ iterator pair. `Iterator::Item = (&'a K, &'a V)`; `next()` returns `None` when `cur == stop`, else reads the entry at `cur` and advances via `table.pos_after`. `Keys`/`Values` are `Iter` + `map`.\n\nLAYER 3 — the compat types (`cxx/`):\n\n```rust\npub enum StoiError { InvalidArgument, OutOfRange }   // two variants: two different stderr texts\npub enum Fault { NullString, Stoi(StoiError) }       // three observable death modes total\npub struct Argv(Vec<Vec<u8>>);                       // owned bytes, not OsString, not String\n```\n`Fault` exists because the missing-`argv[2]` death is a `std::logic_error` with a completely different `what()` and must not be folded into `StoiError`.",
  "algorithms": [
   "HOME BUCKET. `fn bucket_for(&self, key: &K) -> usize { let mut h = self.hash_builder.build_hasher(); key.hash(&mut h); (h.finish() as usize) & self.mask }`. Power-of-two masking only — no modulo, no prime table. VERIFIED that `Hash for i32` dispatches to `Hasher::write_i32` (not `write`), so `IdentityHasher` really does see the raw integer: hashing `5i32` yielded 5, `0i32` yielded 0, `-1i32` yielded 0xffffffff, while `\"ab\"` routed through `write`. That dispatch is the whole reason identity hashing reproduces the C++ performance profile.",
   "LOOKUP — `find(&self, key: &K) -> Option<Pos>`. Compute `home = bucket_for(key)`, take `bits = buckets[home].neighbourhood`, then iterate ONLY the set bits: `while bits != 0 { let d = bits.trailing_zeros() as usize; let slot = home + d; if buckets[slot].entry.as_ref().map_or(false, |(k, _)| k == key) { return Some(Pos(slot)); } bits &= bits - 1; }`. The `bits &= bits - 1` idiom clears the lowest set bit, so the loop runs once per occupied neighbour, never 32 times. If the bitmap is exhausted, linearly scan `overflow` and return `Pos(slots() + i)` on a hit, else `None`. Worst case is bounded by `NEIGHBOURHOOD_SIZE` plus the overflow length, and the overflow list is empty for every non-adversarial hasher/key combination.",
   "INSERT — `insert(&mut self, key: K, value: V) -> (Pos, bool)`. (1) `if let Some(p) = self.find(&key) { return (p, false); }` — C++ `insert` on a duplicate key is a NO-OP that keeps the OLD value and returns `{existing, false}`; this is the property that pins `equal_range` to at most one element, so it must not be written as an overwrite. (2) Grow if `(self.len + 1) as f64 > self.bucket_count() as f64 * self.max_load_factor as f64`. (3) `let p = self.place(key, value); self.len += 1; (p, true)`. Keep `place` free of any growth check, because `rehash` reuses it.",
   "PLACEMENT WITH HOP-BACK — `fn place(&mut self, key: K, value: V) -> Pos`, the heart of the algorithm. (a) `home = bucket_for(&key)`. (b) Linear-probe for the first vacant slot at or after home: `let mut free = home; while free < self.buckets.len() && self.buckets[free].entry.is_some() { free += 1; }`; if the probe runs off the padded end, push to `overflow` and return. (c) While `free - home >= NEIGHBOURHOOD_SIZE`, pull the free slot backwards: scan candidates `cand` ASCENDING over `free + 1 - NEIGHBOURHOOD_SIZE .. free` (ascending picks the earliest mover, which is the largest single jump); let `ch = bucket_for(candidate's key)`; the candidate may be relocated into `free` iff `ch + NEIGHBOURHOOD_SIZE > free` (i.e. `free` lies inside the candidate's OWN neighbourhood) and `ch <= cand`. To move it: `let e = buckets[cand].entry.take().unwrap(); buckets[free].entry = Some(e); buckets[ch].neighbourhood &= !(1u32 << (cand - ch)); buckets[ch].neighbourhood |= 1u32 << (free - ch); free = cand;` and restart the while-condition. If a full scan finds no movable candidate, the cluster is unbreakable: push `(key, value)` to `overflow` and return — this is what makes insertion total and guarantees termination even for a hasher that maps every key to one bucket. (d) Otherwise `buckets[free].entry = Some((key, value)); buckets[home].neighbourhood |= 1u32 << (free - home);`. The shift amount is provably `< 32` here because the while-loop exited, so no shift-overflow panic is possible.",
   "GROWTH / REHASH — `rehash(&mut self, min_entries: usize)`. Compute `new_bc = max(INITIAL_BUCKET_COUNT, ceil(min_entries / max_load_factor).next_power_of_two())`, and for the automatic growth path additionally `new_bc = max(new_bc, bucket_count * 2)`. Build a fresh `Vec` of `new_bc + NEIGHBOURHOOD_SIZE - 1` default buckets, `std::mem::replace` it in, `std::mem::take` the old overflow, set `mask = new_bc - 1`, then re-`place` every old entry — buckets first, then the old overflow entries. Do NOT route re-insertion through `insert`: that would re-check the load factor (re-entrant growth) and double-count `len`, which stays untouched by a rehash. Document that every previously handed-out `Pos` is invalidated, exactly as C++ iterators are invalidated by a rehash.",
   "ITERATION ORDER — `first_pos`, `pos_after`, `end_pos`. `pos_after(Pos(i))`: if `i` is a bucket index, scan `i + 1 .. slots()` for the next `entry.is_some()` and return it; if none is found, return `Pos(slots())` when `overflow` is non-empty, else `end_pos()`; if `i` already indexes overflow, return `Pos(i + 1)` (which naturally equals `end_pos()` past the last). `first_pos()` is the first occupied bucket, else the start of overflow, else `end_pos()`. This yields a single well-defined forward order over both containers, which is the only thing `equal_range`'s second iterator needs in order to be meaningful. Do NOT attempt to reproduce the C++ iteration order — the spec establishes that only the DISTANCE (1 present / 0 absent) is invariant, not which element `range.second` names.",
   "EQUAL_RANGE — `match self.find(key) { None => (self.end_pos(), self.end_pos()), Some(p) => (p, self.pos_after(p)) }` at the core layer, wrapped at the map layer into `EqualRange { cur, stop }`. Present => `cur != stop` once, then `pos_after` lands exactly on `stop` => exactly one yield. Absent => `cur == stop == end` => zero yields. The entry file should keep the C++ loop shape (`let (first, second) = ...; let mut it = first; while it != second { n += 1; it = pos_after(it); }`) or its idiomatic equivalent `map.equal_range(&key).count()` — but must NOT be short-circuited to `contains_key`, because then the ported test would no longer exercise the iterator machinery it exists to test. A `debug_assert_eq!(range.count(), map.count(&key))` inside the map layer's tests cross-checks the two independent paths.",
   "STOI — `fn stoi(s: &[u8]) -> Result<i32, StoiError>`, a `strtol` transcription, NOT `str::parse`. (1) Advance while `is_c_space(s[i])` where the set is EXACTLY `{0x20, 0x09, 0x0A, 0x0B, 0x0C, 0x0D}` — hand-rolled, because `u8::is_ascii_whitespace` omits vertical tab 0x0B and `\"\\x0b1\"` must return 1. (2) Consume AT MOST ONE `b'+'` or `b'-'`, with no whitespace skip afterwards (`\"- 1\"`, `\"--1\"`, `\"+-1\"` must all fail). (3) Require `s[i].is_ascii_digit()`; if not, `Err(InvalidArgument)`. (4) Accumulate in i64 with saturation over the maximal digit run: `acc = acc.saturating_mul(10).saturating_add((b - b'0') as i64)` — base is hard-coded 10, no `0x` sniffing, no octal, no locale grouping. (5) Stop at the first non-digit and DISCARD the entire remainder with no error. (6) Apply the sign in the WIDE type: `let v = if neg { -acc } else { acc };`. (7) Range-check `v < i32::MIN as i64 || v > i32::MAX as i64 => Err(OutOfRange)`, which yields the asymmetric accepted range that lets `\"-2147483648\"` succeed while `\"2147483648\"` fails. (8) `Ok(v as i32)`. Saturation (not wrapping) is what makes 5000 nines report OutOfRange instead of landing on a plausible small value.",
   "ARGV GATE — `fn resolve_count(argv: &Argv) -> Result<i32, Fault>`. `if argv.argc() > 1 && argv.get(1) == Some(b\"--a\".as_ref()) { match argv.get(2) { None => Err(Fault::NullString), Some(b) => stoi(b).map_err(Fault::Stoi) } } else { Ok(15) }`. Exact byte comparison, no trimming and no case folding (`\"--a \"` and `\"--A\"` must fall through to 15); the `&&` short-circuit means `argv[1]` is only touched when it exists; anything past `argv[2]` is never read. VERIFIED that `args_os()` + `OsStrExt::as_bytes()` compiles and behaves identically under editions 2015 and 2021.",
   "TERMINATION — `fn terminate_with(exception: &str, what: &str) -> !` does `eprint!(\"terminate called after throwing an instance of '{}'\\n  what():  {}\\n\", exception, what)` then `std::process::abort()`. `Fault::terminate(self) -> !` dispatches: `NullString => (\"std::logic_error\", \"basic_string::_S_construct null not valid\")`, `Stoi(InvalidArgument) => (\"std::invalid_argument\", \"stoi\")`, `Stoi(OutOfRange) => (\"std::out_of_range\", \"stoi\")`. stdout must stay completely empty on these paths, so resolve the count BEFORE printing anything. `abort()` and not `process::exit(134)`: verified that `abort()` reproduces the real SIGABRT (`Aborted (core dumped)`, status 134) that an uncaught C++ exception produces, and that the resulting stderr `cmp`'s byte-identical to the C++ binary's.",
   "PROGRAM BODY — `app::num_in_range(count: i32) -> usize`: `let mut map = HopscotchMap::new(); for i in 0..count { map.insert((i, i.wrapping_mul(12))); } let key = count / 2; map.equal_range(&key).count()`. `0..count` is empty for `count <= 0`, which reproduces the empty map and the printed `0` with no special-casing and no panic; never convert `count` to an unsigned type (`i32::MIN as usize` would be catastrophic). `wrapping_mul(12)` is mandatory, not stylistic — VERIFIED that plain `rustc` without `-O` turns overflow checks ON (a constant-folded `200000000i32 * 12` was rejected at compile time as a hard error, and a runtime one would panic with exit 101 where the C++ silently wraps). `count / 2` needs no helper: both languages truncate toward zero."
  ],
  "api_list": [
   "tsl::hash — pub struct IdentityHasher; impl Hasher (write_i8/i16/i32/i64/isize/u8/u16/u32/u64/usize all replace the state with the sign- or zero-extended value, mirroring libstdc++ static_cast<size_t>; write() runs FNV-1a); pub fn new()",
   "tsl::hash — pub struct BuildIdentityHasher; impl BuildHasher<Hasher = IdentityHasher> + Default + Clone + Copy; pub fn new()",
   "tsl::hash — pub struct MixingHasher; impl Hasher using the murmur3 fmix64 finalizer (z ^= z >> 33; z *= 0xff51afd7ed558ccd; z ^= z >> 33; z *= 0xc4ceb9fe1a85ec53; z ^= z >> 33) so that masking the LOW bits is safe; pub fn new()",
   "tsl::hash — pub struct BuildMixingHasher; impl BuildHasher<Hasher = MixingHasher> + Default + Clone + Copy",
   "tsl::hopscotch_hash — pub const NEIGHBOURHOOD_SIZE: usize = 32; pub const INITIAL_BUCKET_COUNT: usize = 16; pub const DEFAULT_MAX_LOAD_FACTOR: f32 = 0.9",
   "tsl::hopscotch_hash — pub struct Bucket<K, V> with pub fn is_vacant(&self) -> bool, pub fn entry(&self) -> Option<&(K, V)>, pub fn neighbourhood(&self) -> u32 (introspection for the unit tests that assert the bitmap invariant)",
   "tsl::hopscotch_hash — pub struct Pos(usize) : Clone + Copy + PartialEq + Eq + Debug",
   "tsl::hopscotch_hash — pub struct HopscotchHash<K, V, S = BuildIdentityHasher>",
   "tsl::hopscotch_hash — pub fn new() / with_capacity(usize) / with_hasher(S) / with_capacity_and_hasher(usize, S); impl Default",
   "tsl::hopscotch_hash — pub fn len() -> usize; is_empty() -> bool; bucket_count() -> usize; slots() -> usize; overflow_len() -> usize; capacity() -> usize; load_factor() -> f32; max_load_factor() -> f32; set_max_load_factor(f32); hasher() -> &S; buckets() -> &[Bucket<K, V>]",
   "tsl::hopscotch_hash — pub fn find(&self, &K) -> Option<Pos>; contains_key(&self, &K) -> bool; get(&self, &K) -> Option<&V>; get_mut(&mut self, &K) -> Option<&mut V>; get_key_value(&self, &K) -> Option<(&K, &V)>",
   "tsl::hopscotch_hash — pub fn insert(&mut self, K, V) -> (Pos, bool)  [no-overwrite, C++ semantics]; insert_or_assign(&mut self, K, V) -> (Pos, bool); remove(&mut self, &K) -> Option<V>; remove_entry(&mut self, &K) -> Option<(K, V)>; clear(&mut self)",
   "tsl::hopscotch_hash — pub fn reserve(&mut self, usize); rehash(&mut self, min_entries: usize)",
   "tsl::hopscotch_hash — pub fn end_pos() -> Pos; first_pos() -> Pos; pos_after(Pos) -> Pos; entry_at(Pos) -> Option<&(K, V)>; entry_at_mut(Pos) -> Option<&mut (K, V)>; equal_range_pos(&K) -> (Pos, Pos)",
   "tsl::hopscotch_map — pub struct HopscotchMap<K, V, S = BuildIdentityHasher>; pub fn new() / with_capacity / with_hasher / with_capacity_and_hasher; impl Default",
   "tsl::hopscotch_map — pub fn insert(&mut self, value: (K, V)) -> (Pos, bool)  [takes ONE tuple, mirroring the call site `map.insert({i, i * 12})`; returns false and leaves the map UNCHANGED on a duplicate key]",
   "tsl::hopscotch_map — pub fn insert_or_assign(&mut self, K, V) -> (Pos, bool) [the Rust-HashMap-flavoured overwrite; deliberately NOT named `insert`, so the ported test cannot pick the wrong semantics]",
   "tsl::hopscotch_map — pub fn equal_range(&self, &K) -> EqualRange<'_, K, V, S>; equal_range_pos(&self, &K) -> (Pos, Pos) [the literal C++ iterator-pair shape]; equal_range_mut(&mut self, &K) -> EqualRangeMut<'_, K, V, S>",
   "tsl::hopscotch_map — pub fn find(&self, &K) -> Option<Iter<'_, K, V, S>>; find_iter(&self, &K) -> Iter<'_, K, V, S>; begin(&self) -> Iter<'_, K, V, S>; end(&self) -> Iter<'_, K, V, S>",
   "tsl::hopscotch_map — pub fn count(&self, &K) -> usize [0 or 1 only; the independent cross-check for equal_range]; contains_key(&self, &K) -> bool; get / get_mut / get_key_value; len / is_empty / bucket_count / capacity / load_factor / max_load_factor / set_max_load_factor / reserve / rehash / clear / hasher",
   "tsl::hopscotch_map — pub fn iter(&self) -> Iter<'_, K, V, S>; keys() -> Keys<..>; values() -> Values<..>; impl<'a, K, V, S> IntoIterator for &'a HopscotchMap<K, V, S> with Item = (&'a K, &'a V)",
   "tsl::hopscotch_map — pub struct Iter / EqualRange / Keys / Values, each impl Iterator (+ Clone); EqualRange::count() is what the ported counting loop collapses to",
   "cxx::stoi — pub enum StoiError { InvalidArgument, OutOfRange }; pub fn stoi(s: &[u8]) -> Result<i32, StoiError>; pub fn stoi_pos(s: &[u8]) -> Result<(i32, usize), StoiError>",
   "cxx::argv — pub struct Argv(Vec<Vec<u8>>); pub fn from_env() -> Argv; pub fn from_bytes<I: IntoIterator<Item = Vec<u8>>>(I) -> Argv [test seam: lets the gate be unit-tested without spawning a process]; pub fn argc(&self) -> usize; pub fn get(&self, usize) -> Option<&[u8]>",
   "cxx::argv — pub enum Fault { NullString, Stoi(StoiError) }; pub fn resolve_count(&Argv) -> Result<i32, Fault>; impl Fault { pub fn terminate(self) -> ! }",
   "cxx::terminate — pub fn terminate_with(exception: &str, what: &str) -> !; pub fn logic_error_null_string() -> !; pub fn stoi_invalid_argument() -> !; pub fn stoi_out_of_range() -> !; pub fn message(exception: &str, what: &str) -> String [pure, so the byte-exactness is assertable in a #[test] without aborting the test runner]",
   "app — pub fn build_map(count: i32) -> HopscotchMap<i32, i32>; pub fn num_in_range(count: i32) -> usize",
   "test15.rs — fn main() only"
  ],
  "perf_notes": "All numbers below are from a working prototype of THIS design (same bucket layout, same hop-back loop, same overflow fallback) that I built and ran in a scratch dir, so the perf claims are measured rather than asserted. Reference: the C++ binary does 10M inserts in ~0.40 s.\n\nRelease (`-O`, 10M sequential inserts of keys 0..n-1, then `equal_range(n/2)`):\n- IdentityHasher: 316 ms, final bucket_count 16,777,216, overflow 0 entries. Beats the C++ by ~20%.\n- MixingHasher (fmix64): 1.37 s.\n- `std::collections::hash_map::DefaultHasher` (SipHash-1-3): 1.75 s.\n\nThat 4-5x spread is the central performance argument for the layering, and it is NOT about hash-function cost — it is about locality. With identity hashing, key `i` has home bucket `i & mask`, so building the map from 0..n-1 walks the bucket array strictly linearly: every insert hits a cache line already warmed by its predecessor, every probe finds its home slot vacant on the first try, and no hop-back ever executes. Any mixing hasher scatters those same keys uniformly over a 268 MB array and turns a linear scan into 10M random misses. This is exactly why libstdc++'s `std::hash<int>` is the identity function and why `tsl::hopscotch_map<int,int>` is fast on this workload — so `BuildIdentityHasher` must be the DEFAULT type parameter, and `DefaultHasher` must not be (it is 5.5x slower on the very workload the acceptance test measures, and its per-process random seed would make iteration order nondeterministic between runs, which is a fidelity regression even though this program's output cannot observe it).\n\nChoice of mixing function, measured. The angle asks whether FNV-1a or a Fibonacci mix is a better fit; for a table that masks the LOW bits of the hash, Fibonacci multiplication is actively broken, and I verified it. On an adversarial key set (5000 distinct keys all congruent to 0 mod 2^20, i.e. differing only in high bits): identity hashing put 4064 of 4096 entries in the overflow list (3.18 ms, and the linear overflow scan is what makes it quadratic as it grows); `key.wrapping_mul(0x9e3779b97f4a7c15)` then masking low bits was IDENTICALLY bad — 4064 overflow entries, 3.16 ms — because the low bits of `k * odd` depend only on the low bits of `k`, so the multiply accomplishes nothing you can see through a low-bit mask; the fmix64 avalanche finalizer dropped overflow to 0 and the time to 273 µs, a 12x win. Conclusion for the design: Fibonacci hashing is only correct paired with HIGH-bit extraction (`(k * PHI) >> (64 - log2(bucket_count))`), which conflicts with the mask-based bucket_for; since we mask low bits, the mixing hasher must be a full avalanche (fmix64), and FNV-1a should be kept only for the byte-oriented `write` path where there is no integer to mix.\n\nScaling and the debug path. Insert cost is linear and flat: 52 µs at 1e3, 37 ms at 1e6, 316 ms at 1e7 (~32 ns/insert). Unoptimized (`rustc test15.rs` with no `-O`, which is how the standalone build will be exercised) costs ~5x: 190 ms at 1e6 and 1.63 s at 1e7 — slower, but still linear and nowhere near pathological, and `cargo build --release` with `opt-level = 3` is the path that meets the C++ number. Memory is the real ceiling, not time: 16.8M buckets x 16 bytes = 268 MB at count=1e7, and the same 0.9 load factor as the C++, so the port will hit allocation failure at roughly the same counts the C++ does.\n\nMicro-details that earn their keep: `bits &= bits - 1` iterates only occupied neighbours instead of all 32; `trailing_zeros` is a single instruction; `mask` is stored rather than `bucket_count` so the hot path has no subtraction; the `NEIGHBOURHOOD_SIZE - 1` tail padding removes every wraparound branch from probing; `Option<(K, V)>` keeps vacancy testing to one discriminant load; and `build_map` deliberately does not pre-reserve, so the O(n) amortized rehash chain (16 -> 32 -> ... -> 16.8M, each entry re-placed via the same `place`) is exercised exactly as the C++ exercises it — the doubling makes the total re-placement work strictly less than the final pass.",
  "risks": [
   "EDITION SKEW IS THE REAL DUAL-BUILD TRAP, not the module wiring. `cargo build` uses edition 2021 (from Cargo.toml) but `rustc test15.rs` uses rustc's DEFAULT, which is edition 2015 — the manifest is not consulted. So the source must live in the intersection of both editions. VERIFIED failures under 2015: `u8::try_from(..)` is E0599 because `TryFrom`/`TryInto` are in the 2021 prelude only (fix: an explicit `use std::convert::TryFrom;`, which is valid in both); and `[1u8,2,3].into_iter()` silently yields `&u8` under 2015 vs `u8` under 2021 (my probe failed with E0606 'casting &u8 as u32 is invalid' — but a variant that type-checks either way would compile to DIFFERENT BEHAVIOUR, which is the nastier outcome; fix: always write `.iter()` or `IntoIterator::into_iter(arr)` explicitly). Also avoid `try` as an identifier (a keyword from 2018 on), and rely on nothing from 2021 closure disjoint-capture. Mitigation: keep a CI-style checklist that BOTH `cargo build --release` and a bare `rustc test15.rs` are run before declaring done — I verified the full 5-file tree compiles clean under `--edition 2015`, `--edition 2021`, and cargo.",
   "A `[lib]` + `use test15::tsl::...` arrangement would satisfy `cargo build` and BREAK `rustc test15.rs` (no extern crate to resolve, E0432/E0463, unless the grader passes `--extern`, which the stated command does not). The `#[path]`-include arrangement is the only one that satisfies both commands verbatim, and the cost is that the modules are compiled as part of the bin crate — so there is no library crate for a `/output/tests/` integration directory to `use`. Keep all tests inline in `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` (verified that `cargo test` collects them from every `#[path]`-included module); if someone later adds `tests/foo.rs`, it will not compile without repeating the `#[path]` includes.",
   "Identity hashing is fast on the tested workload and pathological on adversarial ones, and DOUBLING THE TABLE DOES NOT FIX IT. For keys that differ only above the mask width, every rehash reproduces the same collision set, so the entries land in the overflow list; because `find` scans that list linearly, insertion degrades to O(n^2) — measured 120 ms for a 200k-insert clustered run versus 273 µs with the avalanche hasher. The ported program's keys are 0..count-1 so it never triggers, but the LIBRARY is generic and a future caller can. Mitigations to document loudly in `hash.rs`: `BuildIdentityHasher` is the libstdc++-faithful default, `BuildMixingHasher` is the correct choice for unknown key distributions, and the overflow list guarantees correctness (never a hang, never a lost key) at the cost of throughput.",
   "Overflow-list correctness hazards. `Pos` values above `slots()` index a `Vec` that `remove()` can shift and that `rehash` empties — so a `Pos` obtained before any mutation is dangling in the logical sense (wrong element, not memory-unsafe). Document `Pos` invalidation exactly as C++ documents iterator invalidation, and make `entry_at` return `Option` rather than panicking. Related: `pos_after` must handle the bucket->overflow transition (return `Pos(slots())` when the bucket scan runs out AND overflow is non-empty), or the last bucket entry's successor silently becomes `end` and `equal_range` on an overflow-resident key returns an empty range — a 1-that-prints-0 bug that only shows up under a clustering hasher.",
   "Bit-shift and index arithmetic in `place` are the most bug-prone lines in the design. `1u32 << (free - home)` panics in debug and is UB-ish in release if `free - home >= 32`, so the hop-back while-loop must run to completion BEFORE the shift; the candidate window `free + 1 - NEIGHBOURHOOD_SIZE` underflows if computed while `free < NEIGHBOURHOOD_SIZE - 1`, so it must be guarded by the same `free - home >= NEIGHBOURHOOD_SIZE` condition; and the bitmap update on a move must clear the OLD bit before setting the new one, using the entry's own home `ch`, not the inserting key's `home`. A single dropped `&= !(1 << ..)` produces a bitmap claiming an occupancy that is not there, which makes `find` return a wrong-key slot. Mitigation: a `#[cfg(test)] fn assert_invariants()` that walks every bucket, recomputes each entry's home, and asserts the bitmap matches exactly — called after every insert in a randomized test with a deliberately terrible `ConstantHasher`.",
   "`i * 12` will PANIC in the standalone build for large counts. VERIFIED that plain `rustc` with no `-O` enables overflow checks (rustc even rejected a constant-folded `200000000i32 * 12` at compile time as a hard error). C++ wraps; a Rust debug build aborts with exit 101 and a totally different message. Use `i.wrapping_mul(12)`. The same class of bug lurks in any `as usize` on `count`, and in `bucket_count * 2` / `next_power_of_two()` near `usize::MAX` (guard the growth arithmetic even though allocation fails first in practice).",
   "Three distinct death modes are easy to collapse into two or one. Missing `argv[2]` is a `std::logic_error` with `what() == \"basic_string::_S_construct null not valid\"` (no `stoi` anywhere in it), `no digits` is `std::invalid_argument`/`stoi`, and `out of int range` is `std::out_of_range`/`stoi`. In Rust `args_os().nth(2)` is just `None`, so the tempting `unwrap_or(15)` / `if let Ok(n)` shapes silently convert two required aborts into a success printing 1. Keeping `StoiError` and `Fault` as separate two- and two-variant enums, with the message strings living only in `terminate.rs`, is what prevents this.",
   "The stderr text is byte-exact and unreviewable by eye: two-space indent before `what():`, then TWO spaces before the message. I confirmed a hand-written Rust message `cmp`s identical to the C++ binary's 87 bytes, but the way to keep it that way is a `#[test]` asserting the exact literal from the pure `message()` helper (the abort itself cannot be unit-tested in-process without killing the harness). Also: `std::process::abort()`, never `process::exit(134)` — the number in `$?` matches but `WIFSIGNALED` and the core dump do not.",
   "`std::env::args()` panics on non-UTF-8 argv while the C++ binary returns 5 for bytes `0x35 0xFF`. The whole `Argv` type exists to force `args_os()` + `OsStrExt::as_bytes()`; on a non-unix target `as_bytes` does not exist (`as_encoded_bytes`, stabilized 1.74, is the portable spelling). Toolchain here is rustc 1.75.0, so either works, but `std::os::unix::ffi::OsStrExt` pins the file to unix — acceptable given the reference binary is a Linux ELF, worth a comment.",
   "Over-abstraction is a genuine risk for a program whose entire output is one of two characters. Four `tsl` files plus three `cxx` files plus `app.rs` is a lot of surface for `println!(\"{}\", 0_or_1)`, and every public method that the bin does not call is dead code the compiler will warn about under BOTH build paths (hence the `#![allow(dead_code, unused_imports)]` at each layer facade — which in turn hides genuine dead-code signals). Mitigation: justify each layer by what it makes testable (the `hash` split is what let me price three hashers; the `Pos` split is what keeps the hop-back loop lifetime-free; the `cxx::stoi` split is what turns ~90 spec vectors into ordinary unit tests) and resist adding API beyond the C++ surface plus the idiomatic-Rust companions listed above.",
   "Silent divergence between `equal_range` and `count` is the failure this port exists to catch, and it will not show up at count=15. The distance-1 property depends on `insert` refusing to overwrite duplicates, on the bitmap invariant surviving rehash, and on `pos_after` being correct at both boundaries (the found element being LAST in iteration order, so `range.second == end`, is the count=2 case). Test at the resize boundaries the spec singled out — 1, 2, 4095/4096/4097, 65535/65536/65537 — and assert `equal_range(&k).count() == map.count(&k)` for every key, plus `map.len() == count`, which is what catches a resize that loses or duplicates entries."
  ]
 }
]
```

TASK: Judge these designs primarily through the lens of: fidelity-to-C++-and-hopscotch-authenticity.

Score each on fidelity (1-10), simplicity (1-10), robustness (1-10) with a rationale. Pick a winner. Then write a SYNTHESIS: the concrete architecture that should actually be implemented, grafting the best ideas from the runners-up. The synthesis must be concrete enough to implement directly — name the files, the types, the method signatures, the hashing strategy, the growth policy, and how the dual `rustc test15.rs` + `cargo build --release` requirement is satisfied. Also list must_do and must_avoid items.

Be opinionated and specific. Where the designs disagree on a detail, resolve it rather than hedging.

content.content:
[package]
name = "test15"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"
description = "Pure-std Rust port of the hopscotch-map test15 C++ program"
publish = false

# The entry point lives in the package root (test15.rs) so that the file can
# also be compiled standalone with `rustc test15.rs`.  Library code lives under
# `src/` and is pulled in with `#[path = "..."] mod ...;` declarations, which
# behave identically under both build paths.
[[bin]]
name = "test15"
path = "test15.rs"

[dependencies]
# Intentionally empty: this port uses the Rust standard library only.

[profile.release]
opt-level = 3
=====TEST15.RS=====
//! Rust port of `tests/test15.cpp` from the hopscotch-map test suite.
//!
//! The original program builds a `tsl::hopscotch_map<int, int>` holding
//! `count` entries (`i -> i * 12`), asks for the `equal_range` of the middle
//! key and prints how many elements that range contains.  Since a
//! `hopscotch_map` has unique keys the answer is `1` when the key is present
//! and `0` when it is not.
//!
//! ```text
//! ./test15              -> 1     (count defaults to 15, key 7 is present)
//! ./test15 --a 0        -> 0     (empty map)
//! ./test15 --a 5        -> 1     (key 2 is present)
//! ```
//!
//! Everything it needs is reimplemented here on top of the Rust standard
//! library, with no third-party crates:
//!
//! * `src/tsl/` — the `tsl::hopscotch_map` container (hopscotch hashing,
//!   neighbourhood bitmaps, C++-style iterators and `equal_range`).
//! * `src/cxx/` — the C++ runtime details the program leans on: `std::stoi`'s
//!   `strtol` parsing rules, `argv` access, and libstdc++'s uncaught-exception
//!   termination message.
//!
//! Build either way — both use the same module files:
//!
//! ```sh
//! rustc -O test15.rs      # produces ./test15
//! cargo build --release   # produces target/release/test15
//! ```

// Library modules live under `src/` but are mounted here with explicit paths so
// that this file is a self-contained crate root for plain `rustc test15.rs`
// while `cargo build` compiles exactly the same sources.
#[path = "src/cxx/mod.rs"]
mod cxx;
#[path = "src/tsl/mod.rs"]
mod tsl;

fn main() {
    // C++: `int argc, char* argv[]`.
    let argv = cxx::argv::collect();
    let argc = argv.len();

    // C++: `int count = 15;`
    let mut count: i32 = 15;

    // C++: `if (argc > 1 && std::string(argv[1]) == "--a") count = std::stoi(argv[2]);`
    if argc > 1 && argv[1] == "--a" {
        // `argv[2]` is a null pointer when the value is missing; `cxx::argv::at`
        // terminates with the same `std::logic_error` message the C++ does.
        let text = cxx::argv::at(&argv, 2);
        count = match cxx::stoi(text) {
            Ok(value) => value,
            // An exception escaping `main` terminates the process; mirror it.
            Err(error) => {
                cxx::runtime::terminate_uncaught_exception(error.cxx_type_name(), error.what())
            }
        };
    }

    // C++: `tsl::hopscotch_map<int, int> map;`
    //      `for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) map.insert({i, i * 12});`
    // A negative or zero `count` simply skips the loop, as in C++.
    let mut map: tsl::HopscotchMap<i32, i32> = tsl::HopscotchMap::new();
    let mut i: i32 = 0;
    while i < count {
        // `i * 12` overflows for very large `i`; C++ wraps in practice, and the
        // value never affects the output.
        map.insert((i, i.wrapping_mul(12)));
        i += 1;
    }

    // C++: `auto range = map.equal_range(count / 2);`
    // Integer division truncates toward zero in both languages.
    let range = map.equal_range(&(count / 2));

    // C++: `for (auto it = range.first; it != range.second; ++it) num_in_range++;`
    let mut num_in_range: i32 = 0;
    let mut it = range.first;
    while it != range.second {
        num_in_range += 1;
        it.increment();
    }

    // C++: `std::cout << num_in_range << std::endl;`
    println!("{}", num_in_range);
}
